{
  "_comment": "The one home for the rules this project states about how its own texts are written. Every language checker is generated from this file or reads it directly, so a rule's wording, its thresholds, its carve-outs, and its word lists are edited here and nowhere else; a checker that keeps its own copy of a list is drift to fold back. A path written as `~/...` resolves under the agent's own `.claude` directory, and every other path is relative to the repository root. `status` says whether a catcher runs the rule today: `held` means a catcher runs at a named harness event, in a gate, or at a named manual step; `stated-only` means no catcher runs it, which includes a script that exists and is armed nowhere; `claimed-but-absent` means a home names a catcher that does not carry the rule. Inside `catchers`, `partial` means the catcher reads part of the class or part of the surface. Inside `catchers.model`, `where` is a comma-separated list of homes, each one opening with the path of the checker that RUNS the law and naming the mechanism it runs through after `via`; `scripts/gen-language-consumers.py` keys each generated law body by that checker together with a surface, so a checker is handed the laws it actually holds and no others, and a home path under `~/` marks a law the reader's own personal layer owns and the package never ships. `armed` names the event or gate a catcher runs at; a catcher that runs at the PreToolUse event is recorded in `notes`, because the field's vocabulary holds no word for that event. Beside `rules` this file carries three sets the generator prints for a reader: `legend` defines the status words and the arming events, `surfaces` defines each surface with an example and states the block rule, and `question_index` maps a writer's question to the rules that answer it. A rule's `lists` field gives every set the rule names: an entry carrying `source` and `key` is READ out of that JSON file at build time, so the list can never drift; an entry carrying `home` and `members` was copied from a script that holds it in code, and the home is named so a reader knows a change there does not reach the page. To add a rule: append an entry with the next free id, a plain one-sentence `rule`, a `reader_test` a reader can apply to one sentence, the surfaces it binds, its `sources`, and `status` of `stated-only`; then wire or generate the catcher and record it under `catchers`. This file carries Russian pattern text as data, so `scripts/shipped-language-allowlist.json` owes it a `user_language_globs` entry before the shipped-language gate can pass.",
  "legend": {
    "rule_status": {
      "held": "a catcher runs this rule today: at a named harness event, inside a gate, or at a named manual step",
      "stated-only": "no catcher runs this rule, which includes a script that exists and is armed nowhere",
      "claimed-but-absent": "some file names a catcher for this rule, and that catcher does not carry it"
    },
    "catcher_status": {
      "held": "this catcher runs the class it names over the surface it names",
      "partial": "this catcher reads part of the class, or part of the surface",
      "absent": "no catcher of this kind runs the rule"
    },
    "armed": {
      "pre-commit": "a gate refuses the commit until the text changes",
      "pre-push": "a gate refuses the push until the text changes",
      "session-stop-hook": "the session's reply is held at the end of the turn, and a correction is asked for before the text reaches the reader",
      "session-prompt-hook": "the verdict on the previous reply is put in front of the session when the person sends their next message",
      "PreToolUse": "the next tool call is denied, so the correction reaches the reader inside the same turn; a rule armed here records it in its notes, since the `armed` field's vocabulary holds no word for the event",
      "manual": "a person runs the catcher over a file by hand, and nothing runs it on its own",
      "nowhere": "no catcher runs the rule at any event"
    },
    "owner": {
      "script": "a literal check decides a break of this rule on its own",
      "skill": "deciding a break needs a model reading the text for meaning"
    }
  },
  "vocabulary": {
    "note": "The words these rules are stated in. Every page built from this home carries this block, so a reader holding one page holds every word that page uses. A word is defined here when two or more pages need it; a word one page alone needs is defined on that page.",
    "words": [
      {
        "word": "class",
        "definition": "the shape of a mistake: the form it takes wherever it turns up"
      },
      {
        "word": "break",
        "definition": "one place a text falls short of a rule, being a single instance of the class that rule names"
      },
      {
        "word": "catcher",
        "definition": "whatever finds a break: a literal pattern in a script, a model reading for meaning, or a person reading the text"
      },
      {
        "word": "home",
        "definition": "the one file a given fact is written in"
      },
      {
        "word": "the rule home",
        "definition": "the file `guardrails/language-rules.json`, where every rule is edited and nowhere else, and from which every page here is built"
      },
      {
        "word": "rule",
        "definition": "one statement about one class, written as one entry in the rule home"
      },
      {
        "word": "surface",
        "definition": "a kind of text rather than a file, so one file carries several; this project names six, and each rule binds the ones it applies to"
      },
      {
        "word": "stop",
        "definition": "one place a reader could not go on, had to read twice, or had to guess"
      },
      {
        "word": "a blocking stop",
        "definition": "a stop where the reader could not go on with the text, or would have acted on the text wrongly; every other stop is one the reader noticed and read past"
      },
      {
        "word": "register",
        "definition": "the rules a surface's text is held to: which words, which sentence shapes, and how it addresses its reader"
      }
    ],
    "roles": [
      {
        "role": "the writer",
        "definition": "whoever drafts a text: a person, or the agent working for one"
      },
      {
        "role": "the reader",
        "definition": "whoever reads that text afterwards, carrying whatever context they happen to have"
      },
      {
        "role": "a cold reader",
        "definition": "a reader given the text alone, with no repository, no earlier drafts, and no way to ask the writer a question"
      },
      {
        "role": "the maintainer",
        "definition": "whoever builds the catchers and repairs them"
      },
      {
        "role": "the owner",
        "definition": "the one person whose project this is"
      }
    ],
    "closing": "One person often holds more than one of these roles in a day."
  },
  "surfaces": {
    "note": "A surface is a KIND of text, not a file, and one file carries several. The numbered requirements and acceptance criteria of a spec are spec-body; the Context paragraphs and User Story lines in that same file are human-prose. Two rules that would give opposite verdicts on one word therefore never meet on one sentence: they govern different blocks of the file. A text declares one primary surface, and a text published to someone outside the project carries `artifact` as well, with every rule binding either surface in force. Where two rules would still give opposite verdicts on one sentence, that is a defect in these rules: it is recorded and settled here, and a writer is never handed the choice.",
    "list": [
      {
        "name": "spec-body",
        "definition": "the numbered requirements and acceptance criteria of `PRODUCT_SPEC.md`, and the same numbered shape in `ARCHITECTURE.md` and `TEST_MATRIX.md`. A machine reads this text beside the person. The prose paragraphs in those same files are human-prose.",
        "example": "Requirement 297 of `PRODUCT_SPEC.md`, and each acceptance criterion under it."
      },
      {
        "name": "human-prose",
        "definition": "text a person reads to understand something or to decide something. A README, a decision page, a report, a skill's own body, and a documentation page carry it. So do the prose paragraphs inside a spec. A documentation page carries `artifact` as well once it is published outside the project.",
        "example": "`README.md`, and this page."
      },
      {
        "name": "chat",
        "definition": "a message the agent sends the person in conversation, including the narration between tool calls — the checkers read every assistant message since the person's last turn, not the final reply alone.",
        "example": "a reply in a working session."
      },
      {
        "name": "artifact",
        "definition": "a page or file published to someone outside the project: a rendered page, product copy, a public README, a marketing text.",
        "example": "the project's public README as it stands on its repository page."
      },
      {
        "name": "commit",
        "definition": "a commit message: its subject line and its body.",
        "example": "the message on one commit."
      },
      {
        "name": "worker-brief",
        "definition": "the text one agent hands another as its instructions.",
        "example": "the brief a session writes when it starts a background worker."
      }
    ]
  },
  "question_index": [
    {
      "question": "How long may a sentence be, and how much may it carry?",
      "rules": [
        "r08",
        "r44",
        "r45",
        "r46",
        "r73"
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "Which person do I write in, and may I say `you`?",
      "rules": [
        "r24",
        "r25",
        "r26",
        "r75"
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "May I define a term, and where does the definition go?",
      "rules": [
        "r01",
        "r02",
        "r21",
        "r35",
        "r43",
        "r74"
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "A set my sentence points at — must I give its members?",
      "rules": [
        "r07",
        "r08",
        "r45",
        "r73"
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "Which tense, and may I say how the rule came about?",
      "rules": [
        "r30",
        "r31"
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "How many examples may I give, and what kind?",
      "rules": [
        "r41",
        "r43",
        "r61"
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "Structure: when does prose become a list, a paragraph break, or a heading?",
      "rules": [
        "r08",
        "r44",
        "r45",
        "r55",
        "r73"
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "Names: what may I call a thing, and what may I not?",
      "rules": [
        "r02",
        "r03",
        "r04",
        "r07",
        "r11",
        "r23"
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "Numbers and comparisons: when must one be grounded?",
      "rules": [
        "r06",
        "r32",
        "r33"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "r01",
      "name": "an ordinary word carrying a private project meaning",
      "rule": "A word keeps its everyday meaning. A term this project needs holds one glossary entry, written in plain words. The body then uses that term unchanged, with no definition attached.",
      "reader_test": "Would a person outside this project recognize this word, or does the text gloss it in plain words where it first appears?",
      "surfaces": [
        "spec-body",
        "human-prose",
        "chat",
        "artifact"
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "written": "The system shall red a branch whose merge-base sits behind main's tip.",
          "repair": "The system shall refuse a branch whose merge-base sits behind main's tip."
        },
        {
          "written": "a landing",
          "repair": "a change that reached main"
        }
      ],
      "exceptions": [
        "a term the text glosses in plain words at its first use",
        "a term holding a glossary entry the reader meets before the term's first working use"
      ],
      "catchers": {
        "pattern": {
          "status": "partial",
          "where": "guardrails/check-vocabulary.py, scripts/preshow-register-lint.py",
          "reach": "check-vocabulary.py reads the glossary and body of one document named on the command line; preshow-register-lint.py reads any file handed to it line by line against its 23 patterns"
        },
        "model": {
          "status": "held",
          "where": "hooks/register-judge.py via hooks/register_judge_core.py, scripts/preshow-register-lint.py via hooks/register_judge_core.py",
          "law_text": "no coined or jargon term shown to the reader without a plain gloss. A word the reader may not know — a name this project coined for its own machinery, or a piece of domain jargon — dropped into a sentence with no plain-language gloss on first use is banned (e.g. «развилки» shown in chat where the reader was never taught the term). Name the thing in the reader's own plain words, or gloss the term the first time it appears. A widely understood industry-standard word needs no gloss and passes."
        },
        "person": {
          "status": "partial",
          "note": "whether the gloss actually lands for this reader, whether one sentence carries more than one such term, and whether a clarifying question from the reader means a term went ungrounded"
        }
      },
      "armed": [
        "session-stop-hook",
        "manual"
      ],
      "sources": [
        "~/.claude/skills/communicator/references/writing-register.md:43",
        "~/.claude/skills/communicator/references/writing-register.md:22",
        "~/.claude/skills/communicator/references/writing-register.md:25",
        "~/.claude/skills/text-audit/SKILL.md:221",
        "~/.claude/skills/text-audit/SKILL.md:223",
        "~/.claude/skills/text-audit/SKILL.md:111",
        "~/.claude/playbook/personal/profile.md:22",
        "~/.claude/playbook/personal/profile.md:24",
        "docs/spec-format.md:23"
      ],
      "status": "held",
      "notes": "r22, `a term standing bare on its first appearance`, folded into this entry on 2026-07-28 and its id is retired: the two carried one model law word for word, and this entry owns the fact from both sides — the private meaning a word is given, and the gloss the reader is owed at its first appearance. The law text this entry states is the text the register judge is handed; hooks/register_judge_core.py loads it from hooks/language-laws.json and no longer states it. The pre-show lint's pattern list grows by nobody's duty, retracted 2026-07-17 at scripts/preshow-register-lint.py:31; it stands as a free first pass over what it already covers. scripts/register-lint-floor.json:2, ~/.claude/skills/communicator/SKILL.md:466, and ~/.claude/skills/communicator/SKILL.md:216 still state the old growth duty. The writing register lives at ~/.claude/skills/communicator/references/writing-register.md and is named by what it holds; ~/.claude/playbook/personal/profile.md:22 still states the old form, naming a rule count and a path the register left. check-vocabulary.py is armed nowhere.",
      "owner": {
        "by": "skill",
        "why": "Whether an ordinary word carries a private meaning here is a reading of what the word does in its sentence."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "r02",
      "name": "a coined, loan-translated, or respelled word standing where a plain standard word exists",
      "rule": "Where the industry has a word, the text writes the industry's word. A term this project coined is replaced by the standard word, or defined where it first appears. In the reader's own language, a term is written as a real word of that language.",
      "reader_test": "Does a standard word already name this thing? Is the word used here that standard word, written as a real word of the reader's own language?",
      "surfaces": [
        "spec-body",
        "human-prose",
        "chat",
        "artifact"
      ],
      "exceptions": [
        "a minted name for a thing nothing else names, carrying a glossary entry at its first appearance"
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "written": "the door",
          "repair": "the entry point"
        },
        {
          "written": "нога (leg)",
          "repair": "критерий приёмки, открытый пункт (an acceptance criterion, an open item)"
        },
        {
          "written": "краснеет (goes red)",
          "repair": "проваливает проверку (fails the check)"
        },
        {
          "written": "станция (station)",
          "repair": "шаг (step)"
        },
        {
          "written": "дверь (door)",
          "repair": "вход (entry point)"
        },
        {
          "written": "шов (seam)",
          "repair": "стык (join)"
        },
        {
          "written": "сеть (net)",
          "repair": "проверка, механизм (a check, a mechanism)"
        },
        {
          "written": "доска (board)",
          "repair": "список ожидания (waiting list)"
        },
        {
          "written": "дерево (tree)",
          "repair": "рабочая папка проекта (the project's working folder)"
        },
        {
          "written": "живость (liveness)",
          "repair": "работает ли ещё, закончил ли (whether it is still running, whether it finished)"
        },
        {
          "written": "полоса (lane)",
          "repair": "параллельная ветка работы (a parallel line of work)"
        },
        {
          "written": "якорь (anchor)",
          "repair": "код в конце строки (the code at the line's end)"
        },
        {
          "written": "рука (arm)",
          "repair": "часть проверки (one arm of a check)"
        },
        {
          "written": "тело требования (the requirement's body)",
          "repair": "текст требования (the requirement's text)"
        },
        {
          "written": "храповик (ratchet)",
          "repair": "предел, который не растёт (a limit that does not grow)"
        },
        {
          "written": "засеяна (seeded)",
          "repair": "начальные числа записаны (the starting numbers are recorded)"
        },
        {
          "written": "workers read document bodies",
          "repair": "workers read the documents"
        }
      ],
      "lists": [
        {
          "name": "the words replaced by a plain standard term, with the term to use",
          "source": "guardrails/spec-coinages.json",
          "key": "coinages",
          "shape": "map",
          "note": "guardrails/check-vocabulary.py and scripts/spec-style-lint.py both match on this one map; until 2026-07-28 they kept a list each, and a word added to either was invisible to the other"
        }
      ],
      "catchers": {
        "pattern": {
          "status": "partial",
          "where": "guardrails/check-vocabulary.py and scripts/spec-style-lint.py, both reading guardrails/spec-coinages.json; scripts/preshow-register-lint.py",
          "reach": "check-vocabulary.py reads the whole text of one document named on the command line, case-folded and word-boundary matched; spec-style-lint.py reads one file line by line; preshow-register-lint.py adds eight Russian loan-translation patterns and three respelling patterns over any file handed to it"
        },
        "model": {
          "status": "held",
          "where": "hooks/register-judge.py via hooks/register_judge_core.py, scripts/preshow-register-lint.py via hooks/register_judge_core.py",
          "law_text": "no machine dialect in a surface a human reads: a word this project coined where the industry already has one, an English internal term loan-translated word for word into the reader's own language (a calque), or an English coinage respelled in the reader's alphabet. Judge by whether an outside reader, never taught this project's private vocabulary, would meet a word as machinery where meaning belongs. A plain industry-standard word passes, and so does an ordinary word that merely happens to appear inside such a coinage."
        },
        "person": {
          "status": "partial",
          "note": "whether a standard word exists for a thing the text minted a name for, and a loan translation or a respelling nobody has recorded yet, including a coinage born in the writer's own report"
        }
      },
      "armed": [
        "session-prompt-hook",
        "manual",
        "session-stop-hook"
      ],
      "sources": [
        "docs/spec-format.md:21",
        "docs/spec-style.md:27",
        "docs/prose-quality-gate-design.md:19",
        "guardrails/spec-coinages.json:2",
        "scripts/spec-style-lint.py:262",
        "~/.claude/skills/spec-author/SKILL.md:109",
        "~/.claude/skills/text-audit/SKILL.md:223",
        "~/.claude/skills/communicator/references/writing-register.md:46",
        "~/.claude/playbook/personal/profile.md:16",
        "~/.claude/playbook/personal/profile.md:17",
        "~/.claude/skills/live-spec-base/SKILL.md:57",
        "~/.claude/skills/communicator/SKILL.md:264",
        "hooks/chat-law-hook.sh:9",
        "docs/language-defects.md:126",
        "scripts/preshow-register-lint.py:74",
        "scripts/preshow-register-lint.py:96",
        "~/.claude/skills/communicator/SKILL.md:459"
      ],
      "status": "held",
      "notes": "r16, `an internal English term loan-translated into the reader's language`, and r17, `an internal term respelled in the reader's alphabet`, folded into this entry on 2026-07-28 and their ids are retired: one class — a project word reaching the reader where a standard word exists — was split three ways by the form the word happened to take. The word list this entry names was two lists until 2026-07-28: guardrails/spec-coinages.json held six pairs for check-vocabulary.py and scripts/spec-style-lint.py:232 held nine words of its own with no repairs, so a word added to either was invisible to the other reader. They are merged into the one home this entry prints, both readers read it, and the repairs beside the nine were written for this file. The arm that held this rule in chat is retired to attic/midturn-chat-scan.py. It stood before every tool call in the tree. Its refusal landed on whichever call was in flight, a background worker's included. No field of that event tells the two apart. The law stands, and only its machine is gone. The model arm at the pre-show step turns on through PRESHOW_REGISTER_JUDGE and stands off by default (scripts/preshow-register-lint.py:138). attic/chat-calques.json holds the retired scanner's patterns for the same fifteen loan translations the examples give. The profile names three more loan translations — its words for tripwires, for the same incident family, and for a landing — with no repair recorded for any of them, and its list shares no member with that pattern list. A translation after the fact does not repair the sentence; it must be plain the first time. check-vocabulary.py is armed nowhere. The model arm reached documents alone until 2026-07-28. Its only checker was the pre-show lint, which runs over a file. A coined word spoken in conversation met the pattern list, and no meaning read it. A list holds the words someone already caught, and three coinages of one morning walked through it. The turn judge was added here, and it now reads the chat surface for the whole class.",
      "owner": {
        "by": "skill",
        "why": "A list holds the coinages already met; a word coined tomorrow is caught by a reader who knows the standard word."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "r03",
      "name": "a name stacking two nouns with no relation between them",
      "rule": "A name holds one noun. Where two nouns belong together, a verb or a preposition between them carries the relation.",
      "reader_test": "Does this name run two nouns together, and can a reader say how the second relates to the first?",
      "surfaces": [
        "spec-body",
        "human-prose",
        "chat",
        "artifact"
      ],
      "exceptions": [
